1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,320
A Binance engineer interviews for a job

2
00:00:02,360 --> 00:00:03,760
at another company.

3
00:00:03,920 --> 00:00:05,800
First, he speaks with a recruiter.

4
00:00:06,080 --> 00:00:08,000
Then he speaks with someone claiming to

5
00:00:08,040 --> 00:00:09,680
be the company's manager.

6
00:00:09,720 --> 00:00:11,520
He gets a test assignment and downloads

7
00:00:11,600 --> 00:00:13,200
it to his work computer.

8
00:00:13,240 --> 00:00:15,360
But the company doesn't exist.

9
00:00:15,440 --> 00:00:17,520
A few weeks later, his real employer

10
00:00:17,600 --> 00:00:20,640
will lose more than six billion dollars.

11
00:00:20,720 --> 00:00:23,520
The trap looks convincing: the invitation comes

12
00:00:23,600 --> 00:00:25,280
through a real job platform,

13
00:00:25,360 --> 00:00:27,040
the interviews are by video,

14
00:00:27,080 --> 00:00:28,640
and the recruiter even shows him her

15
00:00:28,960 --> 00:00:31,600
account, with personal photos and videos.

16
00:00:31,680 --> 00:00:33,680
It all leads to one thing:

17
00:00:33,760 --> 00:00:35,200
running the code.

18
00:00:35,280 --> 00:00:36,800
As he sets up the assignment,

19
00:00:36,880 --> 00:00:38,800
malware runs alongside it.

20
00:00:39,000 --> 00:00:41,360
Security systems miss it, giving the attackers

21
00:00:41,440 --> 00:00:44,160
access to a computer inside Binance.

22
00:00:44,360 --> 00:00:46,640
According to Binance's preliminary findings,

23
00:00:46,720 --> 00:00:48,400
they use that computer to reach two

24
00:00:48,480 --> 00:00:50,240
more engineers' machines.

25
00:00:50,520 --> 00:00:52,240
Credentials found there give them access to

26
00:00:52,320 --> 00:00:54,560
the system that approves transfers from Binance

27
00:00:54,640 --> 00:00:57,280
wallets, allowing them to authorize transfers to

28
00:00:57,560 --> 00:00:59,093
addresses they control.

29
00:00:59,147 --> 00:01:02,080
On October 29, crypto assets start moving

30
00:01:02,120 --> 00:01:03,627
to those addresses.

31
00:01:03,813 --> 00:01:05,880
Security flags the large transfers.

32
00:01:05,920 --> 00:01:08,427
Binance halts ordinary withdrawals and,

33
00:01:08,453 --> 00:01:10,480
with its partners, tries to freeze the

34
00:01:10,560 --> 00:01:11,800
stolen assets.

35
00:01:11,840 --> 00:01:13,560
But that isn't enough.

36
00:01:13,600 --> 00:01:16,160
Through the compromised system, the attackers manage

37
00:01:16,200 --> 00:01:18,320
to give another program permission to move

38
00:01:18,480 --> 00:01:20,560
crypto assets directly from a wallet on

39
00:01:20,640 --> 00:01:21,640
Ethereum.

40
00:01:21,680 --> 00:01:24,320
That program no longer needs Binance's ordinary

41
00:01:24,400 --> 00:01:25,720
withdrawal system.

42
00:01:25,760 --> 00:01:27,547
Binance has stopped that system,

43
00:01:27,573 --> 00:01:30,000
but the permission recorded on Ethereum remains

44
00:01:30,080 --> 00:01:30,920
active.

45
00:01:30,960 --> 00:01:33,280
Revoking it requires a separate operation on

46
00:01:33,320 --> 00:01:34,840
the blockchain itself.

47
00:01:34,880 --> 00:01:37,600
Turning off withdrawals on Binance's website cannot

48
00:01:37,760 --> 00:01:39,160
cancel that permission.

49
00:01:39,200 --> 00:01:41,440
From the first transfers to the finalization

50
00:01:41,480 --> 00:01:43,800
of the last transaction already sent,

51
00:01:43,840 --> 00:01:45,947
almost four hours pass.

52
00:01:45,973 --> 00:01:47,800
The permission is revoked later,

53
00:01:47,840 --> 00:01:49,000
after midnight.

54
00:01:49,040 --> 00:01:50,960
The stolen crypto assets are valued at

55
00:01:51,040 --> 00:01:54,280
about $6.34 billion.

56
00:01:54,320 --> 00:01:57,520
About $2.85 billion worth of those assets

57
00:01:57,640 --> 00:01:59,573
is frozen or intercepted —

58
00:01:59,600 --> 00:02:00,880
but that does not mean they have

59
00:02:00,960 --> 00:02:02,120
been returned.

60
00:02:02,160 --> 00:02:03,840
Binance says it will cover the loss

61
00:02:03,880 --> 00:02:05,387
with its own funds.

62
00:02:05,413 --> 00:02:08,200
The company he interviewed with never existed.

63
00:02:08,240 --> 00:02:10,000
His real employer had to face the

64
00:02:10,120 --> 00:02:11,040
consequences.

